Title: The Tell-Tale Heart

Credit: Written by

Author: Edgar Allan Poe

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A period-gothic live-action interior. An old bedroom is drowned in darkness. THE OLD MAN, gaunt and white-haired, sleeps beneath a heavy coverlet. THE NARRATOR is never fully seen—only a dark sleeve, restless hands, and a shuttered brass lantern.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

A faint tick sounds somewhere in the house. The unseen hand tightens on the edge of the old man's bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

The old man's eyelid trembles. For an instant, a pale blue eye, filmed like a veil, appears in the darkness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

The hand withdraws from the eye and curls into a fist.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The eye opens again. The narrator's sleeve recoils from it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Daylight enters through the shutters. The old man sits propped in bed. The NARRATOR'S hands tend to him with conspicuous care, smoothing the coverlet and setting everything within reach.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

The light shifts across the room as mornings pass. The narrator's hands adjust the old man's blanket, straighten the bed, and linger only when the old man's eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

Each morning, the narrator enters boldly. The old man looks up. The unseen voice speaks in a hearty, reassuring tone, though the words are indistinct.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, spoke courageously to him in a hearty tone, and inquired how he had passed the night.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALLWAY - NIGHT

MIDNIGHT. The old man's closed door stands at the end of a narrow hall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The latch turns by fractions. The door opens a hair at a time.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head.

The shuttered brass lantern slips through the opening. A dark shape follows, inch by inch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I moved it slowly, very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed.

A long minute hand creeps across a distant clock face. The head does not move.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

The lantern's cover opens the smallest amount. A single thin ray touches the old man's closed eyelid.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I undid it just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

Seven midnights pass in sharp, silent glimpses. Each time, the eye remains shut. Each time, the door closes before dawn.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work. For it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE EIGHTH NIGHT. The NARRATOR'S hand turns the latch more slowly than ever.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

The door opens by another fraction. The old man's room is as black as pitch, the shutters fastened against robbers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph. To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts.

A muted chuckle escapes the darkness. THE OLD MAN shifts suddenly in bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I fairly chuckled at the idea; and perhaps he heard me.

The NARRATOR'S head is inside the room. A thumb slips on the tin fastening.

THE OLD MAN
Who’s there?

The narrator freezes. The old man remains sitting upright, listening. A full hour passes. The old man's breathing is shallow. The walls click with the death watches.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight groan rises from the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well, too.

The narrator's unseen hand presses against its own chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom, deepening, with its dreadful echo, the terrors that distracted me. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

The old man strains toward the darkness, trying to find a harmless explanation.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His fears had been ever since growing upon him. He had been trying to fancy them causeless: “It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor,” or “It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp.” Yes, he had been trying to comfort himself with these suppositions: but he had found all in vain.

A black shadow, cast by the unseen head in the doorway, stretches across the old man's bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim. It was the mournful influence of the unperceived shadow that caused him to feel the presence of my head within the room.

The lantern opens a little—a very little. A ray, thin as a spider's thread, falls upon the old man's eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had waited a long time, very patiently, without hearing him lie down, I resolved to open a little—a very, very little crevice in the lantern. So I opened it—you cannot imagine how stealthily, stealthily—until, at length, a single dim ray, like the thread of the spider, shot from out the crevice and fell full upon the vulture eye.

The eye is open—wide, wide open. It is dull blue, filmed over, and the ray strikes nothing else.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones; but I could see nothing else of the old man's face or person: for I had directed the ray as if by instinct, precisely upon the damned spot.

A low, dull, quick sound begins. It is muffled, like a watch wrapped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense? Now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The lantern remains motionless. The narrator scarcely breathes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. But even yet I refrained and kept still. I scarcely breathed. I held the lantern motionless. I tried how steadily I could maintain the ray upon the eye.

The heartbeat quickens. Louder. Quicker.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant. The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I have told you that I am nervous: so I am.

The old house seems to listen. The narrator's hand trembles, but the ray stays fixed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror. Yet for some minutes longer I refrained and stood still.

The heartbeat swells until it nearly drowns the ticking walls.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell, the lantern flies open. The NARRATOR leaps into the room.

THE OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. The narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done.

The heart beats on beneath the bed, muffled but unmistakable.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This, however, did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall.

The beat slows. Stops.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

The bed is pulled away. The narrator examines the corpse, then presses a hand to the heart and holds it there.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I removed the bed and examined the corpse. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The night wanes. A single candle burns beside the bed. A tub catches everything that might stain the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

A knife rises and falls in silhouette. The corpse is dismembered.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

Three floorboards lift from the chamber floor. The remains disappear between the scantlings. The boards settle back into place, seamless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong.

The narrator's hands inspect the joints. Clean boards. No blood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A CLOCK strikes FOUR. A KNOCK sounds from the street door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o’clock—still dark as midnight.

INT. OLD HOUSE ENTRY HALL - NIGHT

The knocking comes again. The NARRATOR opens the street door with a light, composed gesture.

Three period police officers enter: OFFICER REYNOLDS, broad and gray-mustached; OFFICER HALE, narrow and clean-shaven; and OFFICER MORAN, young and red-haired. All wear dark wool uniforms and caps.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police.

The officers display their badges and look toward the dark rooms beyond.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night; suspicion of foul play had been aroused; information had been lodged at the police office, and they had been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR (O.S.)
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

The narrator smiles. He opens his arms to the house.

NARRATOR (O.S.)
Search—search well.

The officers move through the entry and along the hall. Their lanterns sweep across closed doors, then return.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The officers enter behind the unseen narrator. The old man's treasures remain secure and undisturbed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I took my visitors all over the house. I bade the gentlemen search—search well. I led them, at length, to his chamber.

The officers inspect the walls, the bed, and the untouched treasure. Satisfied, they accept chairs brought into the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

The narrator places his own chair directly above the three concealed planks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues, while I myself, in the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

The officers sit. Their mouths move in easy conversation. Their expressions are mild. The narrator answers cheerily, unseen except for quick hands.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They sat, and while I answered cheerily, they chatted of familiar things.

A faint ringing threads through the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted.

The ringing becomes a low, dull, quick beat beneath the officers' voices.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct: it continued and became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The narrator's hand grips the arm of the chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No doubt I now grew very pale; but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased—and what could I do?

The heartbeat is now unmistakable, like a watch enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

The narrator rises. The chair legs scrape against the floorboards.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased.

The narrator paces to and fro with heavy strides. The officers remain seated, chatting pleasantly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, as if excited to fury by the observations of the men—but the noise steadily increased.

The narrator seizes the chair and grates it across the boards. The pounding rises over everything.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh God! what could I do? I foamed—I raved—I swore! I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased.

The officers smile at one another and continue their pleasant conversation.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder—louder—louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not?

The narrator's hands claw at his head. The pounding shakes dust from the seams of the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!—this I thought, and this I think.

The officers' smiles remain calm. The narrator's breath breaks into a strangled gasp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But anything was better than this agony! Anything was more tolerable than this derision! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die!

NARRATOR (O.S.)
Villains!—dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here! It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The room holds still. Beneath the boards, the pounding continues. The officers stare down at the hidden floor.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
